POSIX signal-state shims (Phase 2 chunk 1+2)
============================================

Wire-format translators between musl's `struct k_sigaction` /
8-byte kernel-shape `sigset_t` and the kernel-side native types,
plus the kill/tkill generation entrypoints.

Chunk 1 (sigaction/sigprocmask/sigpending) only stores and queries
state; chunk 2 (kill/tkill) sets pending bits but the delivery side
isn't wired until chunk 3, so handler bits stored here have no
observable effect yet. The work that sees these shims fire is libc
startup probing default disposition plus the C test program in
`slix/test/sigact.c`.

This file is part of `module oskit.posix` — the same module as
`shim.lsysl`. Cross-file references (`shim_read_le_i64`,
`shim_write_le_i64`, errno vals) resolve via shared module scope.
The dispatch entries (134/135/136/214/360) live in
`shim.lsysl::posix_dispatch`.

    module oskit.posix

    import oskit.kernel.{kernel_sigaction_set, kernel_sigmask_set,
        kernel_sigpending_get, kernel_sig_consume_pending,
        kernel_sig_send_to_thread, kernel_sig_send_to_process,
        kernel_perform_sigreturn}


sys_rt_sigaction
----------------

**Important:** musl does NOT pass its userspace 152-byte `struct
sigaction` directly to `rt_sigaction` — it wraps it in a compact
32-byte kernel-shape `struct k_sigaction` (see
`slix/musl/src/internal/ksigaction.h` and the per-arch override at
`slix/musl/arch/x86_64-slix/ksigaction.h`). slix-musl defines
`SA_RESTORER` for both aarch64 and x86_64, so both arches use the
same kernel-shape layout:

```
offset 0:  handler   (8B, fn ptr or SIG_DFL=0 / SIG_IGN=1)
offset 8:  flags     (8B, unsigned long — high 32 always 0)
offset 16: restorer  (8B, fn ptr to libc __restore_rt)
offset 24: mask      (8B = unsigned mask[2], LE;
                      low u32 = signals 1..32,
                      high u32 = signals 33..64)
```

`sigsetsize` arg passed by musl is `_NSIG/8 = 8` for slix-musl.
We accept anything ≥ 8 and ignore.

Likewise `sigset_t` for rt_sigprocmask / rt_sigpending is the
kernel-shape 8-byte mask (NOT musl's 128-byte userspace
`sigset_t`).

SYS_RT_SIGACTION (283). Args: a0=signo, a1=*act (or 0), a2=*oldact
(or 0), a3=sigsetsize. Returns 0 on success, -EINVAL on bad signo
or SIGKILL/SIGSTOP override attempt.

    sys_rt_sigaction(signo: int, act_va: i64, oldact_va: i64, sigsetsize: int) -> i64
        if signo <= 0 || signo > 64
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if sigsetsize < 8
            return i64(-EINVAL)

        var new_h: i64 = i64(0)
        var new_f: i32 = i32(0)
        var new_m: i64 = i64(0)
        var new_r: i64 = i64(0)
        var install = 0
        if act_va != i64(0)
            val ap: *byte = *byte(act_va)
            new_h = shim_read_le_i64(ap, 0)
            new_f = i32(shim_read_le_i64(ap, 8) & i64(0xFFFFFFFF))
            new_r = shim_read_le_i64(ap, 16)
            new_m = shim_read_le_i64(ap, 24)
            install = 1

        var old_h: i64 = i64(0)
        var old_f: i32 = i32(0)
        var old_m: i64 = i64(0)
        var old_r: i64 = i64(0)
        val rc = kernel_sigaction_set(signo, new_h, new_f, new_m, new_r, install,
                                      &old_h, &old_f, &old_m, &old_r)
        if rc == -1
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if rc == -2
            return i64(-EINVAL)

        if oldact_va != i64(0)
            val op: *byte = *byte(oldact_va)
            shim_write_le_i64(op, 0,  old_h)
            shim_write_le_i64(op, 8,  i64(int(old_f)) & i64(0xFFFFFFFF))
            shim_write_le_i64(op, 16, old_r)
            shim_write_le_i64(op, 24, old_m)

        i64(0)


sys_rt_sigprocmask
------------------

SYS_RT_SIGPROCMASK (285). Args: a0=how, a1=*newset (or 0),
a2=*oldset (or 0), a3=sigsetsize. `how` is SIG_BLOCK=0 /
SIG_UNBLOCK=1 / SIG_SETMASK=2 (matches musl).

    sys_rt_sigprocmask(how: int, newset_va: i64, oldset_va: i64, sigsetsize: int) -> i64
        if sigsetsize < 8
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if how < -1 || how > 2
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        var newmask: i64 = i64(0)
        var actual_how = -1   // query-only by default
        if newset_va != i64(0)
            val np: *byte = *byte(newset_va)
            newmask = shim_read_le_i64(np, 0)
            actual_how = how
        val oldmask = kernel_sigmask_set(actual_how, newmask)
        if oldset_va != i64(0)
            val op: *byte = *byte(oldset_va)
            shim_write_le_i64(op, 0, oldmask)
        i64(0)


sys_rt_sigpending
-----------------

SYS_RT_SIGPENDING (284). Args: a0=*set, a1=sigsetsize. Writes the
caller-thread's pending bitmap (8 bytes, kernel-shape) and returns 0.

    sys_rt_sigpending(set_va: i64, sigsetsize: int) -> i64
        if sigsetsize < 8
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if set_va == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val pending = kernel_sigpending_get()
        val sp: *byte = *byte(set_va)
        shim_write_le_i64(sp, 0, pending)
        i64(0)


sys_kill
--------

SYS_KILL (214). Args: a0=pid, a1=signo. POSIX `kill(pid, sig)`.
Sends `signo` to every thread in the target process; SIGKILL is
the synchronous-terminate fast path. `signo == 0` is the
"does this pid exist?" probe — kernel reports ESRCH if not.
Returns 0 on success, -EINVAL on a bad signo, -ESRCH on a free /
zombie pid. The "broadcast to all threads" choice is the
chunk-2 stopgap — chunk 3's delivery side will pick one
eligible thread when handlers actually fire.

Pid <= 0 forms (kill(0,_) → process-group; kill(-1,_) → broadcast
to every process the caller may signal) are not yet supported;
they return ESRCH for now. Process groups are a Phase-3 follow-up.

    sys_kill(pid: int, signo: int) -> i64
        if pid <= 0
            return i64(-ESRCH)
        if signo < 0 || signo > 64
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val rc = kernel_sig_send_to_process(pid, signo)
        if rc == -1
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if rc == -3
            return i64(-ESRCH)
        i64(0)


sys_tkill
---------

SYS_TKILL (360). Args: a0=tid, a1=signo. Linux's per-thread
`tkill(tid, sig)` — POSIX raise(3) compiles to tkill(gettid(),
sig) in musl, so wiring this also wires raise(). SIGKILL on
any thread still tears down the whole process (POSIX says
SIGKILL is process-scoped at the implementation level).

    sys_tkill(tid: int, signo: int) -> i64
        if tid < 0
            return i64(-ESRCH)
        if signo < 0 || signo > 64
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        val rc = kernel_sig_send_to_thread(tid, signo)
        if rc == -1
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if rc == -3
            return i64(-ESRCH)
        i64(0)


sys_rt_sigreturn
----------------

SYS_RT_SIGRETURN (287). No args. Invoked by libc's `__restore_rt`
trampoline when a signal handler returns. The slix-musl restore
shims (`slix/musl/src/signal/{aarch64-slix,x86_64-slix}/restore.s`)
load `287` into the syscall reg and trap. The kernel reads the
saved `ucontext` off the user stack and rewrites the trap frame so
the next return-to-user resumes the interrupted code with its full
register state and pre-delivery sigmask. Returns the saved
syscall-result register (RAX on x86_64, x0 on aarch64); the
dispatcher writes it to the trap frame's result slot.

    sys_rt_sigreturn() -> i64
        kernel_perform_sigreturn()


sys_signalfd4
-------------

SYS_SIGNALFD4 (336). Args: a0=fd (-1 to create a new one,
otherwise the existing signalfd to retarget), a1=*mask (8-byte
kernel-shape sigset), a2=sigsetsize, a3=flags. Returns the
allocated fd on create, the input fd on retarget, -EMFILE if
no slot is free, -EINVAL on bad args.

Linux's `signalfd_siginfo` is 128 B; only `ssi_signo` (first
4 B) is populated here — chunks 1-4 don't track `si_pid`,
`si_uid`, or `si_code`, and there's no realtime-signal queue
that would carry `si_value`. Callers that need richer siginfo
will get it in the SA_SIGINFO follow-up; for the signalfd
contract POSIX only requires `ssi_signo` to be accurate.

Userspace flow: `sigprocmask(SIG_BLOCK, &mask)` first (so the
delivery walker doesn't run the handler before we get to
read), then `signalfd4(-1, &mask, 8, flags)`, then `read()`
or `epoll_wait()` on the returned fd.

    val POSIX_SFD_NONBLOCK = 2048
    val POSIX_SFD_CLOEXEC  = 524288

    sys_signalfd4(fd: int, mask_va: i64, sigsetsize: int, flags: int) -> i64
        if sigsetsize < 8
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if mask_va == i64(0)
            return i64(-EFAULT)
        val mp: *byte = *byte(mask_va)
        val mask = shim_read_le_i64(mp, 0)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        if pid >= 16
            return i64(-EINVAL)
        var pfd = fd
        if pfd < 0
            pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_SIGNALFD, 0)
            if pfd < 0
                return i64(-EMFILE)
            var fd_flags = 0
            if (flags & POSIX_SFD_NONBLOCK) != 0
                fd_flags = fd_flags | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
            posix_fd_set_flags(pfd, fd_flags)
        else
            if posix_fd_kind(pfd) != POSIX_FD_SIGNALFD
                return i64(-EINVAL)
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + pfd
        posix_fd_table[idx].signalfd_mask = mask
        i64(pfd)
